{
  "evidence": "TEXT_G: End austerity; halt welfare cuts and benefit freeze; CPI-uprate benefits; scrap Bedroom Tax; oppose two-child cap; restore Universal Credit work allowance; NHS Scotland budget +£2bn; strengthen mental health; retain free prescriptions; fair public sector pay; make the real Living Wage the legal minimum; ban exploitative zero‑hours contracts; strengthen gender pay gap reporting and equal pay audits; protect pensions Triple Lock and oppose raising State Pension Age; limit immigration detention to 28 days and confirm EU nationals’ rights; stand up for Scotland/devolution/choice on independence; tougher tax collection and a 50p top rate. TEXT_C: +£11bn/year for NHS and social care; +£500m for mental health; restore nursing bursaries; scrap hospital parking; maintain triple lock; raise Carer’s Allowance; scrap bedroom tax; ban zero‑hours in home care; ‘British jobs first’; net migration near zero; five‑year contributions before benefits/NHS; tougher NHS eligibility for non‑contributors; sovereignty-first Brexit tests; +20,000 police, restore stop and search, +7,000 prison officers; repeal Human Rights Act and leave ECHR; disband sharia councils and ban face coverings; raise tax thresholds; cut foreign aid and scrap HS2.",
  "reasoning": "Given the electorate’s strongest weights on Care and Equality, TEXT_G’s focus on expanding NHS and mental health provision, reversing welfare cuts, legalizing a living wage, and protecting vulnerable groups (including EU residents) maps closely to those priorities while not leaning heavily on Authority or Sanctity. TEXT_C strongly emphasizes national loyalty, law‑and‑order authority, cultural/procedural purity, and contribution-based proportionality—areas that, while present, are less weighted in the profile—so despite its care provisions it aligns less overall with the stated moral priorities.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
